Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) or porous coordination polymers (PCPs) are a kind of crystalline microporous materials constructed by periodic connection of inorganic and organic units,[1] which have shown great potential in fields such as gas storage/separation, luminescence, magnetism, catalysis or electronic/ionic conduction.[2] MOFs are typically prepared as crystalline powders. However, for the better application in many fields, MOFs thin films are more favorable.[3] In recent years, increasing research interest has been focused on the design and preparation of hetero-structured multi-layer MOF thin films.[4] The final target in this research field is to build a cascade of MOF layers with different properties for advanced applications and so produce a complex function that cannot be achieved by a single MOF layer. Hetero-structured MOF-on-MOF thin films have been prepared by the stepwise liquid-phase epitaxial (LPE) growth of one MOF layer on another MOF layer, which involves one-to-one coordination bonding at the interface.[5] Typically, the second MOF layer is required to match the structure or at least the lattice in the specific crystal surface of the first MOF layer.
Unfortunately, MOFs with different properties generally possess distinct crystal unit cell parameters and even topological structure. Consequently, a method that can integrate different functional MOF layers independently of the lattice matching issue is extremely desirable but challenging. Numerous multilayer MOF thin films have been successfully prepared and structurally characterized, and many of their applications have been reported.[5] Cascading the properties of different MOF layers, such as gas adsorption/separation and chemiresistive sensing properties, to realize an advanced device function however has not been reported to date.
Van der Waals (vdW) integration is a bond-free assembly method that can physically integrate different materials with the weak vdW interactions between them.[6] Since it does not involve a chemical bonding at the interface of two materials, vdW integration is not limited to structurally matched materials. This method of integration has enjoyed great success in the preparation of the hetero-junction of two-dimensional (2D) atomic crystals.[7] In this work, we report a convenient vdW integration method which enables assembly of two latticemismatched MOF layers, Cu-HHTP $(\mathsf{H H T P=}2,3,6,7,10,11$ - hexahydrotriphenylene) and Cu-TCPP $(\mathsf{T C P P}\ =\ 5,10,15,20-$ tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin). With this method, MOF layers with rationally designed properties were assembled in sequence, as shown schematically in Figure 1 to produce an enhanced device function for the first time.
The application of a MOF-on-MOF thin film as a chemiresistive sensing material was used as an example to demonstrate its advantages in realizing a complex device function. MOFs have attracted growing interest as a new type of chemiresistive sensing material due to their tunable composition, ultrahigh porosity and the large amount of active sensing sites.[8] Interestingly, our research found that semiconductive Cu-HHTP thin film has an obvious response to benzene at room temperature (RT) but it has an even higher reponse to polar gas molecule, such as $N H_{3}$ . To demonstrate the advantages of MOF-on-MOF thin films, Cu-TCPP layers which can selectively suppress the penetration of $N H_{3}$ were vdW-integrated on CuHHTP. Due to the smooth conjunction of the molecular sieving layer and chemical sensing layer, the prepared Cu-TCPP-onCu-HHTP thin film showed, to the best of our knowledge, the highest sensitivity and tunable selectivity to benzene at RT amongst all reported recoverable chemiresistive materials.
Cu-HHTP has a honeycomb-like porous structure which is constructed by an AB stacking of extended hexagonal 2D π- conjugation layers to form a hcb topology (Figure 1 and S1,and Scheme S1a). Cu-HHTP thin films were prepared by employing a layer-by-layer spray LPE method on -OH functionalized substrates with preprepared Au interdigital electrodes (Figure 1, for details see Supporting Information (SI)).[8d] The thickness of Cu-HHTP was well controlled to an average $\mathord{\sim}1.5$ nm per cycle (Figure S2).[8d] Subsequently, Cu-HHTP thin films were activated by successive immersions in N,N-dimethyl formamide (DMF) and ethanol at RT (details see SI).
Cu-TCPP has a sql topology, which is different that of CuHHTP (Figure S3). To solve the lattice mismatching issue, a “modular assembly” stamping method which can assemble MOF nanosheets into a highly oriented thin film was employed to van der Waals-integrate Cu-TCPP on Cu-HHTP (Figure 1 and Scheme S1b).[9] Crystalline Cu-TCPP nanosheets with fewmolecular-layer thickness were prepared by an one-pot reaction of metal ions and ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{T C P P}}$ (for details see SI, Figures S4 and S5). Then, the nanosheets are dispersed in ethanol to form a translucent suspension which was coated dropwise onto the surface of water in a beaker where it reassembles to form a continuous layer (Figure 1). Next, Cu-TCPP layer was transferred onto the surface of Cu-HHTP layer by stamping. Upon repetition of these steps, the thickness of Cu-TCPP layer increased with the number of cycles.
Cu-TCPP- $\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{X C}}.$ -on-Cu-HHTP-yC thin films, where x (0-10) and y (10-50) are the growing cycles, have been successfuly prepared (Figures 2, S2, and S6-S8). The scanning electronic microscopy (SEM) and atomic force microscopy (AFM) images of the plane view of Cu-TCPP-xC-on-Cu-HHTP-20C $\scriptstyle(x=0$ , 5, 10) are shown in Figures 2a-2f. It was observed that Cu-TCPP-0Con-Cu-HHTP-20C, i.e. Cu-HHTP-20C, has a smooth and crackfree surface with thickness of ${\sim}30$ nm (Figure S2) and roughness less than $2.5~\mathsf{n m}$ . Transmission electron microscope (TEM) and selected area electron diffractions (SAED) measurements (Figure $2{\mathfrak{g}}$ and S9) of Cu-HHTP thin film peeled off from the substrate demonstrated its pure crystal phase and also revealed its oriented growth along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ axis due to the lack of (00l) diffraction.[8d] When stamping Cu-TCPP onto Cu-HHTP layer, the thickness and surface roughness of Cu-TCPP- $\cdot\mathsf{x C}$ -onCu-HHTP-20C increased linearly and reaching ${\sim}61$ and $\sim14.2$ nm, respectively, when $\textsf{x}=\textsf{10}$ (Figure 2h). Comparatively, its roughness increased much more slowly than its thickness, indicating smooth growth of the Cu-TCPP layer on the Cu-HHTP layer. Figure 2i shows the in-plane and out-of-plane patterns of Cu-TCPP-10C-on-Cu-HHTP-20C which were measured using a grazing-incidence X-ray diffraction (XRD) technique (for details, see SI). The in-plane and out-of-plane peaks can be indexed as (hk0) and (00l), respectively, in the reported crystal structure of Cu-TCPP, which indicates the oriented growth of a Cu-TCPP layer along the [001] direction (Figure S3). Due to the weak diffraction of Cu-HHTP in the bottom layer, its peak was not observed. The combination of TEM measurement of Cu-HHTP and XRD measurement of Cu-TCPP revealed Cu-TCPP-10Con-Cu-HHTP-20C is highly oriented and the one-dimensional channels in both MOFs are perpendicular to the substrate. After removal of part of the Cu-TCPP layer by scotch tape and scratching off part of Cu-HHTP layer with a tweezer, the stagelike structure of Cu-TCPP-10C-on-Cu-HHTP-20C was clearly visible in its AFM image (Figure S8).
Cu-TCPP, an electronic insulator, was selected as the gas molecular sieving layer because its 2D structure possesses abundant coordination-unsaturated Cu ions which have much stronger interaction with $N H_{3}$ than with benzene (Figure 1). CuHHTP is a $\mathsf{p}$ -type semiconductive MOF material with a conductivity of $0.2\ \mathsf{S\ c m}^{-1}$ at RT. [8d, 10] It was introduced as gas sensing signal transducing layer. Therefore, cascading the properties of Cu-TCPP and Cu-HHTP in this way may result a highly sensitive and selective gas sensing material. The sensing performances of thin films with Cu-TCPP-xC-on-Cu-HHTP-yC were evaluated at RT by depositing them on a sapphire substrate which contains pre-prepared gold electrodes (for details, see SI).[8d, 11]
Figure 3a shows the response of Cu-TCPP-0C-on-CuHHTP-yC, i.e. Cu-HHTP-yC, with different growing cycles to 100 ppm $N H_{3}$ and benzene, respectively. It was found that among all tested thin films, Cu-HHTP-20C has the required high response to both gases, and thus it was selected to be coated with CuTCPP for further investigation. As shown in Figure 3b, after being coated by Cu-TCPP layer with 5 cycles, Cu-HHTP-20C showed a remarkably decreased response to $N H_{3}$ , from $230\%$ to $94\%$ , and a slightly enhanced response to benzene from $134\%$ to $153\%$ . The selectivity $(\mathsf{S}~=~R_{\mathsf{b e n z e n e}}/R_{\mathsf{N H3}})$ of Cu-HHTP-20C towards benzene and $N H_{3}$ was reversed by depositing Cu-TCPP onto the Cu-HHTP-20C. The thicker Cu-TCPP layer further enhanced this reversed selectivity which approaches saturation after 10 cycles (Figures 3c, S10 and S11). An improvement in S of up to $\sim250\%$ was obtained by Cu-TCPP-10C-on-Cu-HHTP20C compared with Cu-HHTP-20C. Cu-TCPP-10C-on-CuHHTP-20C also showed good response-recovery properties to benzene at RT and its response increased with increments in the benzene concentration from 1 to 100 ppm (Figure 3d). Its response and recovery times were estimated to be 1.53 and 10.72 min, respectively (Figure 3e). The theoretical limit of detection (LOD) to benzene was calculated to be 0.12 ppm from the linear log-log plots of responses vs. concentration by setting response $=10\%$ (Figure 3e). Notably, Cu-TCPP-10C-on-CuHHTP-20C exhibited the highest response toward benzene amongst the reported recoverable RT chemiresistors (Figure 3f).[12]
To reveal the detailed sensing process, Zn-TCPP which is the isostructure of Cu-TCPP, was coated on the surface of CuHHTP with the same procedures (Figure S12). Compared with Cu-TCPP-10C layer, the Zn-TCPP- $\mathsf{\Pi}\cdot\mathsf{x c}$ $(\mathsf{x}=5,10)$ layer caused little change on the sensitivity and selectivity of Cu-HHTP-20C toward to $N H_{3}$ and benzene (Figure 4a and S13). These results demonstrated the critical role of the Cu-TCPP molecule sieving layer in reversing the selectivity of Cu-HHTP. Compared with $Z n^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ has stronger interaction with $N H_{3}$ at the axial positions of the octahedral coordination geometry due to its Jahn-Teller distortion, which blocks the permeation pathway of $N H_{3}$ .[8e, 13] Consequently, Cu-TCPP is better at suppressing the permeation of $N H_{3}$ over Zn-TCPP. Further data analysis revealed that Cu-TCPP-5C and Cu-TCPP-10C layers reduced ${\sim}78\%$ and $\sim90\%$ permeation of 100 ppm $N H_{3}$ , respectively (Figure 4b). After diffusing through the Cu-TCPP layer, analyte gases interact with Cu-HHTP. The subsequent charge transfers from gas molecules to p-type Cu-HHTP led to a current change.[8d, 14, 15] Under flowing dry air, the analyte gas was desorbed from Cu-HHTP resulting in current recovery.
In summary, for the first time, the different properties of the MOF layers in hetero-structured MOF-on-MOF thin films were cascaded and synergistically provide an enhanced device function. The highly oriented MOF-on-MOF thin film was prepared by van der Waals-integrating the second MOF layer onto the first MOF layer. This method is convenient and avoids the need to consider the lattice matching condition required by earlier methods for the growth of MOF-on-MOF thin films. Benefiting from this method, a molecular sieving MOF layer, CuTCPP, was integrated onto a chemiresistive sensing MOF layer, Cu-HHTP, with controlled thickness. By the properties cascading, Cu-TCPP-on-Cu-HHTP not only realized the highest response to benzene of all reported RT chemiresistive sensing materials, but also reversed the selectivity of Cu-HHTP toward benzene and its strongly interfering molecule, NH3. Further, through principal component analysis (PCA) of the data collected with Cu-TCPP-xC-on-Cu-HHTP-20C, 5 typical human breath biomarkers can be clearly detected and distinguished. This work enriches the preparative chemistry for hetero-structure MOF-on-MOF thin films. The order integration of the various properties of MOFs achieved in this work creates a new dimension with which made-to-order functions of MOF materials for advanced device applications can be realized.
